{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>And Elohim said: Let the earth sprout vegetation.</b> He decreed that in the generations of the earth there should be a power of vegetation that produces seed, so that the species would endure forever. And it is possible that the verse hints at the earth mentioned in the first verse — that a vegetative power is drawn from it, and behold, the elemental forces were drawn off from its power to their kinds, and from them sprouted grasses and trees in the Garden of Eden, and from them in the world at large. This is what they said (Genesis Rabbah 11:9): On the third day He created three creations — trees, grasses, and the Garden of Eden. And they further said (Genesis Rabbah 10:6): There is no herb below that does not have a constellation in the firmament that strikes it and says to it, \"Grow!\" — as it is written (Job 38:33), \"Do you know the statutes of heaven, or do you impose their rule on earth?\" — a magistrate. And He said that all this should be \"after its kind,\" and this constitutes the prohibition of kilayim (mixed species), for one who sows them denies the power of the act of Creation; and I will explain it further with the help of HaShem (Leviticus 19:19). And Rabbi Shlomo wrote: \"desheh (vegetation) eisev (herb)\" — not desheh in the sense of eisev, and not eisev in the sense of desheh; for desheh denotes the clothing of the earth with herbage, and one does not say \"desheh of so-and-so,\" whereas every root standing on its own is called eisev. But this statement of his is not correct, for if so, the word desheh would not be used in the plural, yet the Sages say (Ḥullin 60a): \"He grafted two species of desha'im — what is the meaning?\" and the Rav himself mentions desha'im. Rather, desheh is the small growth, and eisev is the large one that produces seed; therefore Scripture says \"let the earth sprout (tadshei)\" and it would not be possible to say \"let it herb (ta'asiv).\" And every small growth from the earth is called desheh, even among trees; therefore תַּדְשֵׁא הָאָרֶץ עֵץ פְּרִי may be understood as applying also to the tree, for Scripture did not say \"let the earth sprout desheh eisev and bring forth עֵץ פְּרִי,\" but rather it is all in the sense of sprouting; and so too (Joel 2:22), \"for the pastures of the wilderness have sprouted, for the tree has borne its fruit.\" And I am astonished that Scripture does not mention barren trees, and how it commanded only עֵץ פְּרִי alone. Perhaps it was this that prompted our Rabbis to say (Genesis Rabbah 5:9): Even barren trees produced fruit. And if so, we would say that due to the curse, \"cursed be the ground\" (Genesis 3:17), they became barren. And it is possible that the meaning of the verse is: let the earth bring forth vegetation — herbage producing seed and tree-making fruit — and behold, at the outset He decreed concerning herbs of the wilderness and barren trees; and from what it says, \"עֹשֶׂה פְּרִי אֲשֶׁר זַרְעוֹ בוֹ\" (Genesis 1:12), we learn that all the trees grew from their seed, even though the practice with some of them is to plant from the branch."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {0 "The word סרק (serak), meaning barren/non-fruit-bearing, is rendered throughout as 'barren'; in the phrase 'herbs of the wilderness' (עשבי הסרק) the idiom is preserved literally."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-21T22:13:22.899787446Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}